            <ArticleTitle>Posttraumatic headaches in civilians, soldiers, and athletes.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>283-303</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ncl.2013.11.010</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0733-8619(13)00135-7</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Posttraumatic headaches are one of the most common and controversial secondary headache types. After mild head injury, more than 50% of people develop a postconcussion syndrome which has been controversial for more than 150 years. Headache is estimated as present in 30% to 90% of patients after mild head injury. Most headaches are of the tension type, although migraines can increase in frequency or occur acutely or chronically de novo. A review is provided of headaches in civilians, soldiers after blast trauma, athletes, and post-craniotomy including pathogenesis. The treatments are the same as for the primary phenotypes.</AbstractText>
